  "summary": "New preprints couple mechanics, kinetics and reaction-diffusion to chromatin and condensate organization, with a coarse-grained DDX3X study as a looser…",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is multiscale modeling in which a driving process is coupled explicitly to the organization it shapes. One preprint combines chromatin elasticity, water transport and epigenetic reaction-diffusion in a non-equilibrium model to predict hydration-driven phase organization. Another integrates biochemical kinetics with polymer modeling to infer cohesin turnover, motion and encounter rules from sister chromatid folding signatures. A third uses molecular simulations to show that kinase-phosphatase cycling (CK1δ, calcineurin) drives self-sustained oscillations of TDP-43 LCD condensates. The recurring element is that dynamical or kinetic parameters are tied to structural or phase outcomes through the coupled models. The DDX3X study, which pairs coarse-grained simulations with deep learning to describe ATP-independent, entropy-driven dsRNA unwinding, is a different system and shares only the simulation-based framing.",
